  "decisions": [
    {
      "at": "2026-11-01T16:40:17Z",
      "choice": "applied",
      "earlier": [],
      "finding": "R1-F1",
      "reason": "Valid. The record now gives the flag syntax, a value-less flag given once, and a rule every bridge meets that does not depend on one vendor; the choice of a fixed or derived feature list stays with the plan."
    },
    {
      "at": "2026-11-01T16:40:21Z",
      "choice": "applied",
      "earlier": [],
      "finding": "R1-F2",
      "reason": "Valid. The reasoning count is now an informational figure that no caller adds to or subtracts from another count, so the shape is complete without relying on a fact not yet checked against the program."
    },
    {
      "at": "2026-11-01T16:40:25Z",
      "choice": "applied",
      "earlier": [],
      "finding": "R1-F3",
      "reason": "Valid. The record now binds what an endpoint record must hold and refuse, how the host is chosen, and the tests against a fake server; the exact record format is left to the spec of the server bridge."
    },
    {
      "at": "2026-11-01T16:41:25Z",
      "choice": "applied",
      "earlier": [],
      "finding": "R2-F1",
      "reason": "Valid. The core now lists seven options including the server option with its grammar, and says recognizing an option is not accepting it, so a refusal by a program bridge is the same everywhere and not an unknown-option error."
    },
    {
      "at": "2026-11-01T16:41:29Z",
      "choice": "applied",
      "earlier": [],
      "finding": "R2-F2",
      "reason": "Valid. The guarantee now allows only the endpoint record credential in the mechanism the record names, and still forbids sending anything else the caller or prompt supplies and forbids logging or exposing the credential."
    },
    {
      "at": "2026-11-01T16:43:01Z",
      "choice": "applied",
      "earlier": [],
      "finding": "R3-F1",
      "reason": "Valid. The claim that the server bridge keeps text on the machine was too strong. The record now says the bridge reaches only the host the owners store record names, that the location class is the owners declaration and is not verified, and that a remote host in a record sends the text there. Requiring loopback would break the stated use of a model machine on the owners network."
    },
    {
      "at": "2026-11-01T16:43:07Z",
      "choice": "applied",
      "earlier": [],
      "finding": "R3-F2",
      "reason": "Valid. The transport now follows the scheme so a TLS field cannot disagree with it, an authenticated endpoint must use https unless the host is a literal loopback address, and a record that breaks this is refused. Unauthenticated http on the owners network stays allowed with a warning from the diagnostic."
    }
  ],
